    "q-154670": {
      "dom_id": "step-q154670",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Which of the following equations has *no* solution?\n\nI. $|5-x|=7$\n\nII. $1-|5-x|=5$\n\nIII. $|5-x|=-1$",
      "worked_solution": "An absolute value equation has no solution if, when the absolute value is isolated on the left side, the right-hand side is negative.\n\nWith that in mind, let's look at each equation in turn:\n\n- Equation I has a solution. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is non-negative.\n\n- Equation II *has no solution*. To see this, we first isolate the absolute value on the left-hand side as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-|5-x| & =5 \\\\ -|5-x| & =4 \\\\ |5-x| & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the right-hand side is *negative*, this equation has *no* solution.\n\n- Equation III *has no solution*. The absolute value is already isolated on the left-hand side, and the right-hand side is *negative*.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
