    "q-215513": {
      "dom_id": "step-q215513",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Written in ascending order, the solutions to $4-|2x-5|=3$ are $x=${{field-1}} \nand $x=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-|2x-5| & =3 \\\\ -|2x-5| & =-1 \\\\ |2x-5| & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following two cases:\n\n- If $|2x-5|=2x-5,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =1 \\\\ 2x-5 & =1 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $|2x-5|=-(2x-5),$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|2x-5| & =1 \\\\ -(2x-5) & =1 \\\\ -2x+5 & =1 \\\\ -2x & =-4 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the solutions are $x=2$ and $x=3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
